The reason for this is probably that they haven't yet had the time for
this. After all, steeds are relatively new to the game, and I guess that
they first wanted to get the errors out of the main steed code, before
adding all sorts of funny little extras.

> -having a #jump option if it doesn't already exist; maybe requiring at
> least basic skill

Make that "skilled", I guess.

> -having a lengthened hunger timeout when riding, if it doesn't
> already exist

Seems reasonable. Then again, you should increase the mount's food
consumption by the same amount.

> -having a weight-sharing effect (might be a little tricky to program,
> but, logically, having your steed carry heavy weights you couldn't
> manage by yourself is pretty much what having one SHOULD be about).

I think you're right here. Every creature has a certain maximum carrying
capacity (just like you do), which could be used here. Of course, this
means that while you're sitting on it, it wouldn't be able to carry much
more than you and your equipment. If you wouldn't sit on it, it should
be able to carry a lot in its saddlebags.

> Every game I've ever played I play more than 95% of the game either
> "burdened" or "stressed", even WITH a blessed BoH, and +5GoS.

You're really doing something very wrong, then. I'm a real pack-rat, and
always carry everything I find to my stash, somewhere near an altar
(even if there's only a single altar in the main dungeon, as in my last
game, where I carried everything out of Gehennom to level 4!).

Even then, I am never burdened. The moment I get burdened, I drop
something. There are always lots of junk items that you may easily lose.

> It'd be
> nice to be able to *carry* things like crystal balls, ice boxes,
> tinning kits, and spellbooks instead of having to drop them because
> they're simply too heavy (i.e., when your blessed BoH already weighs
> 1200 units). Wouldn't it be nice to be able to retrieve and *use*
> some of those land mines in Fort Ludios, in addition to the gold?

Why would you want to take a crystal ball with you? A few scrolls can
almost always achieve the same effect.

An ice box has *deliberately* been made very heavy to make sure that
only the most foolhardy adventurers will take one with them (because it
would be very unbalancing with regard to sacrificing), even if the
contents alone would make this almost impossible anyway. I don't think
you can put ice boxes in a bag of holding, by the way...

As for tinning kits, they are so useful for obtaining intrinsics (for
example, when you meet 3 giants), that they are heavy as a game balance
measure, I guess.

Land mines are indeed quite heavy, too heavy I guess.

Ans spellbooks are only needed every 20000 turns. It takes hardly five
minutes to move to *any* dungeon level and back, normally, so just
putting them in a stash somewhere shouldn't be any more trouble than
running around burdened all the time.

My estimate is that you've not got your priorities right. The game is
fooling you into carrying junk around by making you find too much stuff.

> -having certain critters that only certain classes/races/alignments
> can use, like allowing gnomes to ride some relatively small monsters,
> or letting only celibate females ride unicorns, or letting only flame
> mages ride red dragons, etc.

I agree that the riding system could use some fine-tuning. You could
also use the skill level for this, I suppose (anyone can ride a pony,
but you'd need basic for a horse, and skilled for a warhorse, for
example).

> -aside from some things like mumaks, mastodons, and baluchitheria
> which might already be allowed, adding a few new steed types (the "u"
> monster class really doesn't have much in it) like hippocampi or
> hippopotami on some watery special levels, for water travel, or a
> skeletal horse for necromancers

Could be nice, although I wonder what you'd do with that hippo *after*
you'd crossed Juiblex' Swamp. I don't like leaving pets behind (unless
I'm chaotic); I always feel guilty about that.

> You get the idea...there are many ways that steeds could be improved
> upon so they really represent something more than just another
> novelty pet. At *least* consider the weight-sharing thing. While I
> can certainly imagine that there are purists who look at the 1000
> unit weight limit as an integral and important part of the game, and
> managing one's inventory within those limits to be part of the
> role-playing, but it's always struck me as a little inconsistent,
> since most of the fantasy literature, even D&D, is realistic enough
> to allow beasts to carry stuff *for* the heroes.

As said before, this would matter mostly if a steed's carrying capacity
was at least a bit greater than an adventurer's carrying capacity, since
it would have to carry not only your character's equipment, but also
your character. How much does your character weigh, by the way?

